  "title": "Kourovka Notebook Problem 21.26",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $G$ be a non-trivial finite group and let $p_1,\\ldots,p_k$ be the distinct prime divisors of $|G|$. For each $i$, let $H_i$ be a Sylow $p_i$-subgroup of $G$. Is it true that there exists an element $x\\in G$ such that for all $i$ the subgroup $H_i\\cap H_i^x$ is inclusion-minimal in $\\{H_i\\cap H_i^g:g\\in G\\}$?",
